# Min-el Backend Fixes — Deployment Guide

This covers the three CRITICAL issues that could only be fixed with a real
backend: the reports PIN, subscription/trial creation, and Chapa payments.

## 1. One-time setup

```bash
npm install -g firebase-tools      # if you don't have it
firebase login
cd /path/to/your/project           # the folder containing this functions/ dir
firebase init functions            # choose "Use an existing project" -> min-eli
                                    # when it asks to overwrite functions/index.js
                                    # and package.json, say NO — keep these files
```

## 2. Install dependencies

```bash
cd functions
npm install
```

## 3. Set your Chapa SECRET key (not the public key)

Never hardcode this, never put it in a client file, never commit it to git.

```bash
firebase functions:secrets:set CHAPA_SECRET_KEY
# paste your CHASECK_TEST-... (or CHASECK-... in live mode) when prompted
```

> If you pasted your secret key in a chat conversation with me to get this
> built, rotate it now from your Chapa dashboard (Settings → API) before
> going live — treat anything typed into a chat as no longer private.

## 4. Deploy the functions

```bash
firebase deploy --only functions
```

This deploys:
- `verifyReportsPin` — checks the PIN server-side, sets a 30-minute custom claim
- `startTrial` — the only place a trial/plan can be created, one per company
- `initializeChapaPayment` — starts a Chapa checkout using your secret key
- `chapaWebhook` — the **only** place a subscription is ever marked "paid"; verifies Chapa's signature before trusting anything

## 5. Register the webhook URL with Chapa

After deploying, copy the exact URL Firebase prints for `chapaWebhook`
(should be `https://us-central1-min-eli.cloudfunctions.net/chapaWebhook`) and
paste it into your Chapa Dashboard → Settings → Webhooks.

## 6. Deploy the Firestore rules

```bash
firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
```

The `firestore.rules` file in the project root is a **starting point** built
from what the client code actually reads/writes — not a guarantee. Look for
every `ADJUST ME` comment and confirm it matches how you actually want data
shared between owners, sellers, and branches before going live.

## 7. Test before going live

- In Chapa's test mode, complete a full trial → pay → webhook flow and
  confirm `subscriptions/{companyId}.status` flips to `active`.
- Try entering a wrong Reports PIN 3 times as a seller account and confirm
  the tab stays locked, then enter the right one and confirm sales data
  actually appears (not just the tab).
- Try calling `startTrial` a second time for the same company (e.g. from the
  browser console) and confirm it's rejected with "already used its free trial".

## What's still manual / out of scope

- **Video calling** (`meeting-settings.html`) is a static mockup with no
  backend at all — wiring up real calls needs a video SDK like Jitsi or
  Agora, which is a feature build, not a bug fix.
- **Code duplication** across the 5 HTML files (Firebase config, utility
  functions) — consider extracting a shared `js/firebase-init.js` once
  you're ready to restructure the front end; didn't touch this since it
  risks breaking things without a bundler/build step in place.
- **Tailwind CDN → CLI build** — same reasoning; a build-step change, not a bug fix.
